faq:
  - q: How Much Does Engine Repair Cost vs. Replacement?
    body: >-
      Every engine repair estimate at Duft Auto comes in writing before work begins, so the cost of fixing an engine is clear before a decision gets made. A repair addressing a single failed component — a valve cover gasket, a sensor, a timing chain tensioner — costs a fraction of a full engine replacement and often restores normal operation for years of continued use. Duft Auto walks through the repair-versus-replace tradeoff directly with the customer once diagnostic testing confirms the failed component, not after the invoice arrives. Engines with an isolated mechanical fault rarely justify replacement over targeted repair.
  - q: How Do I Know My Engine Needs Repair?
    items:
      - Check engine light staying on or flashing
      - Knocking or ticking sound, especially at cold start
      - Rough idle, stalling, or hard starts
      - Visible oil or coolant leaks under the vehicle
      - White or blue smoke from the exhaust
  - q: What Does the Engine Repair Process Involve at Duft Auto?
    body: >-
      A visit starts with a scheduled appointment — no walk-in wait, no guessing when a bay opens up. Auggie or a certified technician runs an OBD-II scan paired with a physical inspection, and a compression test when a fault points to a specific cylinder. Findings get explained in plain language, with a written estimate covering parts and labor before any work starts. Straightforward repairs — sensors, gaskets, ignition components — often complete same day, while internal component work such as a timing chain gets a realistic timeline up front.
